💡 𝗕𝗮𝘀𝗵 𝗧𝗶𝗽: 𝗘𝗮𝘀𝘆 𝗗𝗲𝗳𝗮𝘂𝗹𝘁𝘀 𝘄𝗶𝘁𝗵 ${𝗩𝗔𝗥:-𝗱𝗲𝗳𝗮𝘂𝗹𝘁}

Sometimes when you write a script, the user may forget to give an argument.
Instead of breaking, you can tell Bash: “use this instead.”

That’s what ${VAR:-default} does.

👉 Example:

𝘋𝘌𝘚𝘛_𝘋𝘐𝘙="${2:-$𝘏𝘖𝘔𝘌/𝘣𝘢𝘤𝘬𝘶𝘱𝘴}"

If the user gives a 2nd argument → use it.

If not → save the backup in ~/backups.

Run this:

./𝘣𝘢𝘤𝘬𝘶𝘱.𝘴𝘩 /𝘦𝘵𝘤 /𝘮𝘯𝘵/𝘴𝘵𝘰𝘳𝘢𝘨𝘦  # Saves to /mnt/storage
./𝘣𝘢𝘤𝘬𝘶𝘱.𝘴𝘩 /𝘦𝘵𝘤        # Saves to ~/backups
